Infectious Mononucleosis
Young Adult & Adolecence
Exudative pharyngitis, Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, Profound fatigue, Atypical lymphocytosis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-022
Reason for Visit: Acute inability to swallow oral fluids, severe throat pain, and impending dehydration secondary to massive tonsillar swelling.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Sibling
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a young adult with a progressively worsening sore throat over 5 days, now culminating in an absolute inability to swallow saliva, muffled voice, and presyncope upon standing. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rule out impending airway compromise (e.g., peritonsillar abscess) and to facilitate immediate parenteral symptom control and hydration prior to the next regular GP session.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My throat is entirely swollen shut, swallowing my own spit feels like swallowing razors, and I haven't been able to drink water for 14 hours."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a two-week history of profound fatigue and malaise. Five days ago, a high persistent fever developed alongside a rapidly worsening sore throat. The patient describes severe throat pain radiating to the ears. Over the last 24 hours, the tonsillar enlargement has become so severe that it is causing mechanical dysphagia, leading to the current state of acute dehydration. The patient also notes painful swelling in the back of the neck. Previous over-the-counter paracetamol use has been completely ineffective.
Functional Status: Bedbound, lethargic, spitting saliva into a cup to avoid the agonizing pain of swallowing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration requiring hospital admission; airway patency concerns.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 112 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, toxic, and speaks with a thick, "hot potato" voice. Mild periorbital oedema (Hoagland sign) is noted.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals severe bilateral tonsillar hypertrophy meeting in the midline (kissing tonsils). There is a thick, confluent greyish-white exudate covering both tonsils. Palatal petechiae are visible at the junction of the hard and soft palate. No uvular deviation or unilateral soft palate swelling. Neck examination demonstrates marked, tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Abdominal exam reveals mild left upper quadrant fullness without overt peritonism, suggestive of splenomegaly. The clinical presentation is highly indicative of the diagnostic tetrad: exudative pharyngitis, posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, profound fatigue, and anticipated atypical lymphocytosis on a blood smear.
Environmental Observations: Multiple tissues and a spit cup are present; room is darkened.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. No recent amoxicillin exposure.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Infectious Mononucleosis (Glandular Fever) with impending dehydration - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, symmetric nature of the tonsillar swelling and the central, non-deviated position of the uvula.

Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent posterior (rather than purely anterior) cervical lymphadenopathy, the prolonged prodrome of profound fatigue, and the presence of splenomegaly.

Acute Diphtheria - Ruled out/unlikely given the patient's fully immunized status and the exudate being easily mobilized rather than forming a tightly adherent, bleeding pseudomembrane.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Medical risk of airway compromise/dehydration)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe infectious mononucleosis complicated by critical bilateral tonsillar hypertrophy causing absolute dysphagia and early dehydration. The presence of a "hot potato" voice and tachycardia necessitated an immediate, urgent physical evaluation to confidently exclude an asymmetric peritonsillar abscess requiring surgical drainage. Delaying assessment would have rapidly led to severe hypovolaemia and potential airway obstruction. The urgent home visit allowed for safe airway evaluation, the administration of potent parenteral corticosteroids to rapidly shrink the lymphoid tissue, and the initiation of supervised, structured oral rehydration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Dexamethasone 8mg IM stat to rapidly decrease severe tonsillar oedema and restore the ability to swallow fluids. Administered Paracetamol 1000mg PR to provide antipyresis bypassing the inflamed oropharynx. Ordered urgent EBV serology (Monospot/EBV panel) and FBC to confirm atypical lymphocytosis via the regular GP tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent traumatic splenic rupture, strictly avoid all contact sports or heavy lifting for the next four weeks, recognizing that the viral infection has made the spleen physically fragile and prone to bursting under pressure. To safely restore hydration, take exactly one small sip of ice water every 5 minutes rather than attempting large gulps, utilizing the cold temperature to provide local anesthesia while slowly reversing the fluid deficit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 24-48 hours to assess airway patency, review serology, and monitor hydration status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000) is required if the patient develops stridor (noisy, high-pitched breathing), feels physically unable to move air into the lungs, or experiences sudden, severe left-sided abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically presumed as glandular fever; formal blood tests are required for definitive confirmation.
Clinical Course: The steroid injection will significantly shrink the tonsils within 4 to 6 hours, allowing you to drink normally, but the extreme fatigue may persist for several weeks or months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you start to make a high-pitched squeaking noise when breathing in, if you feel you cannot get enough air, or if you develop a sudden, sharp pain under your left rib cage.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your tonsils are so swollen from the viral infection that they are physically blocking your throat. The steroid injection acts rapidly to shrink this swelling, which is the only way to allow you to drink water safely and stay out of the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Sip ice water continuously once the swelling goes down. Do not play any sports or lift heavy objects for a month, as your spleen is currently enlarged and could rupture easily if hit.

